description.narrative:
Flooding remains one of Nairobi’s most persistent and costly urban challenges, causing repeated loss of life, displacement, infrastructure damage, and major economic disruption. Recent flood events have displaced over 110,000 people across Kenya and contribute to annual damages estimated at more than USD 500 million. Despite ongoing efforts to strengthen drainage systems, preparedness, and disaster response, implementation has been constrained by limited financing and fragmented institutional coordination.

In response, a consortium of Dutch technical partners developed a concept in March 2025 for a flagship Urban Flood Early Warning System aimed at strengthening Nairobi’s flood resilience. One year later, severe flooding in March 2026 has once again demonstrated the urgency and relevance of establishing a robust and operational flood early warning capability for the city.

Via a letter dated 6  February 2025, The Principal Secretary for Water, Sanitation and Irrigation formally requested Dutch collaboration and technical support to strengthen Kenya’s flood resilience through the development of an advanced Flood Early Warning System (FEWS), particularly for vulnerable urban areas such as Nairobi and Mombasa.

The request highlights the growing frequency and severity of flooding in Kenya, which continues to result in significant loss of life, displacement, and economic damages estimated at approximately USD 500 million annually. Recognising the Netherlands’ global expertise in flood management and early warning systems, the Ministry invited Dutch public and private sector partners to collaborate on the initiative. The requested areas of support encompaased the following:

Technical expertise to identify and design feasible FEWS solutions;
Integration of hydrological, meteorological, remote sensing, and sensor network data systems;
Development of public-private partnership models for FEWS operation and maintenance;
Capacity building and training for Kenyan institutions and technical staff;
Financial support through Dutch financing instruments and partnerships.

At the same time, Kenya has significantly increased its national commitment to multi-hazard early warning systems through the EW4All national launch and broader engagement under the WMO African EW4All framework. With strong political backing, dedicated budget allocations, and growing momentum around climate resilience, there is now a strategic opportunity for a high-impact Dutch–Kenyan partnership to advance an integrated flood early warning system for Nairobi.

The request further aligns the proposed collaboration with Kenya’s disaster risk reduction priorities, the National Water Resources Strategy, and the global Early Warnings for All (EW4All) initiative. The Ministry specifically encouraged collaboration with Dutch knowledge institutes and companies, including Royal HaskoningDHV, HKV, Deltares, TU Delft, and other Dutch partners active in water resilience and climate adaptation.
